So basically, we are having a Microsoft Excel and it is having sheets and it is having cells.
We need to identify or define how these are connected as an OOPs concept.


You are having multiple cells which are having some values and there is another cell which is using some function.
Let's say it is using some function like addition of two cells.
So, if one is getting updated then the linked set should be automatically updated, right?
Automatically updated. How do you implement such a design? We need to design it using Java design pattern.

interface Observer {
    void update();
}

interface Subject {
    void addObserver(Observer o);
    void removeObserver(Observer o);
    void notifyObservers();
}

class Cell implements Subject {
    private int value;
    private List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();

    public void setValue(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        notifyObservers(); // auto-update dependents
    }

    public int getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    public void addObserver(Observer o) { observers.add(o); }
    public void removeObserver(Observer o) { observers.remove(o); }
    public void notifyObservers() {
        for (Observer o : observers) o.update();
    }
}

class FormulaCell extends Cell implements Observer {
    private Cell cell1;
    private Cell cell2;

    public FormulaCell(Cell c1, Cell c2) {
        this.cell1 = c1;
        this.cell2 = c2;

        // Subscribe to changes
        c1.addObserver(this);
        c2.addObserver(this);

        update(); // initial calculation
    }

    @Override
    public void update() {
        int newValue = cell1.getValue() + cell2.getValue();
        super.setValue(newValue); // triggers its own observers if any
    }
}


Describe a data structure for which getValue(int index), setValue(int index, int value), and setAllValues(int value) are all O(1)
Below is the classic interview answer to this problem.
We need a data structure supporting all three operations in O(1):
	•	getValue(int index)
	•	setValue(int index, int value)
	•	setAllValues(int value)  ← the tricky part

This is not possible with a normal array, because setAllValues() would take O(n).
So we use a lazy global assignment trick.

class O1Array {
    private int[] value;              // stores individual values for each index
    private int[] timestamp;          // stores the last update time for each index
    private int globalValue;          // value set by the most recent setAllValues()
    private int globalTimestamp;      // timestamp of last setAllValues() call
    private int currentTime;          // global counter to track order of operations

    public O1Array(int size) {
        value = new int[size];        // initialize value array
        timestamp = new int[size];    // initialize timestamp array
        globalTimestamp = -1;         // no global update yet, so set to -1
        currentTime = 0;              // start time counter at 0
    }

    // O(1)
    public void setValue(int index, int val) {
        value[index] = val;           // store the value directly at the index
        timestamp[index] = currentTime++; // record current timestamp and increment time
    }

    // O(1)
    public void setAllValues(int val) {
        globalValue = val;            // update the global override value
        globalTimestamp = currentTime++; // record timestamp of this global operation
    }

    // O(1)
    public int getValue(int index) {
        // If this index was updated AFTER the last global update
        if (timestamp[index] > globalTimestamp) {
            return value[index];      // return the specific value stored for this index
        } else {
            return globalValue;       // otherwise return the global value
        }
    }
}